{"title":"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Seventeen people correspond by mail with one another - each one with all\nthe rest. In their letters only three different topics are discussed. Each pair\nof correspondents deals with only one of these topics. Prove that there are\nat least three people who write to each other about the same topic.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Lemma: Consider a complete graph with 6 vertices colored with 2 colors. There exists a monochromatic triangle.\n</p><p>Proof: Consider one vertex and all connections leading out from it. Call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72f539a3dc0cea9899543319d030a34ca6c33b0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />. It has 5 edges coming out from it. By the Pigeonhole Principle, there are at least 3 of the same color. Call this color red. Call those vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/2/ec2fe4ffdca06f7e4f7e2df05465a894eda12eb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4aea33a4b581b8eee9877168a8e99017f68fc7e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/9/279205a9afa893ae0a8d3cd2cfb3f5293e93ce74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" />. If any of the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/6/316967c21108f245011ce9391d799f1276f33547.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_2V_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"34\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/9/b497797657783f51d4d545e952e0a0ae2ef98cde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_2V_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"15\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e928155614383dfa07db2dc32a1bd15961f2752.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_3V_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"15\" /> are red, then we have a monochromatic triangle with vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72f539a3dc0cea9899543319d030a34ca6c33b0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> and the other two that are also red. If they are all the other color, then we have a monochromatic triangle with vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/2/ec2fe4ffdca06f7e4f7e2df05465a894eda12eb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4aea33a4b581b8eee9877168a8e99017f68fc7e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/9/279205a9afa893ae0a8d3cd2cfb3f5293e93ce74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Main Problem: Represent these people as vertices on a connected graph with 17 vertices and colored with 3 colors, one corresponding with each topic. So this problem is reduced to showing that on a connected graph with 17 vertices and colored with three colors, there exists some monochromatic triangle. Look at an arbitrary vertex. Call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72f539a3dc0cea9899543319d030a34ca6c33b0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />. Look at the 16 other vertices that it is connected to. By the Pigeonhole Principle, there are at least 6 vertices connected to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72f539a3dc0cea9899543319d030a34ca6c33b0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> that are all one color. Call this color 1. If any of the connections inbetween these six vertices are in color 1, then we are done. If none of them are color 1, we know that that there are only 2 colors in those 6 vertices. By Lemma 1, we know that there is a monochromatic triangle in those 6 vertices. So we are done.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let there exist a graph of 17 vertices. The diagonal conjoining two vertices is to be colored one of red, blue, or green. \n</p><p><b>Lemma:</b> There exists a monochromatic triangle among the edges.\n</p><p><b>Proof of the Lemma (A,B):</b>\n</p><p><b>Proof A:</b>\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ramsey%27s_Theory&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Ramsey&#39;s Theory (page does not exist)\">Ramsey's Theory</a> finishes the Lemma, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/d/82da698912c93bbf29a61676af369bed3244a693.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(3,3,3) = 17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"116\" height=\"20\" /> exists and thus there exists a monochromatic triangle. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>Proof B:</b>\n</p><p>We finish with Pigeonhole Principle. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/4/4047f5fbfae5fe598ceec3ec3122e8cd6f26f092.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{17(14)}{2} = 119$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"108\" height=\"40\" /> diagonals in a 17-gon. Then, each are to be colored red, blue, or green. We prove that there exists a monochromatic triangle for any configuration without Ramsey's Theory. Consider an arbitrary vertex, which has 16 diagonals emerging from it. Then, by Pigeonhole, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/6/a36d1ab62c2e08447820f27739a541abe84c090e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lceil \\frac{16}{3} \\rceil = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"73\" height=\"38\" /> diagonals with the same color, say, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/9/e49cbb3a8187d322cf569ccd418b0f8192bf0103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$VA_1, VA_2, \\dots, VA_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"158\" height=\"18\" /> are all, without loss of generality, red. If some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/7/e6709958d970625f2504210e8cbde672f1e328da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_iA_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> is red then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/f/61f89f7371825e6fcd32640d66f505a25d63c97e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$VA_iA_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"56\" height=\"19\" /> is monochromatic. Otherwise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/7/e6709958d970625f2504210e8cbde672f1e328da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_iA_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> is blue or green. Take WLOG <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> and notice it has 5 edges emanating from it. At least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/7/45785c9135fc329cd5195a0ab826fd2618d62746.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lceil \\frac{5}{2} \\rceil = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"64\" height=\"38\" /> of them have the same color. Say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/a/c3a8b34b00c7a0dc049cd400264163978e7f62f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2A_3, A_2A_4, A_3A_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"145\" height=\"18\" /> have the same color. That then forms a monochromatic triangle. Otherwise, they are green, and that forces another monochromatic triangle. The lemma has been proven. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>1964 IMO P4:</b>\n</p><p>The <b>Lemma</b> finishes the problem. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO\" title=\"1964 IMO\">1964 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1964_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1964 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261016182050\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.025 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.041 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 181/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1238/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4849/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.847      1 -total\n 55.98%    7.192      1 Template:IMO_box\n 42.94%    5.517      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:8267:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261016182050 and revision id 291573. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}